WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:10.000
This is Chapter 3: Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure announcements.

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.836
When a platform the agency doesn't
run is patched on a Saturday night,

5
00:00:13.836 --> 00:00:16.849
the people who own what runs
on it should hear from the

6
00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:19.863
provider's own record, not
from the help desk on Monday.

7
00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:24.774
We'll start from what Oracle's documentation
gives you, see what it leaves to you,

8
00:00:24.774 --> 00:00:27.117
then read how the kit fills the record,

9
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:29.988
and walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:31.563
Two notes before we begin.

11
00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:34.924
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

12
00:00:34.924 --> 00:00:38.820
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.907
Why Oracle Cloud?

14
00:00:43.907 --> 00:00:46.707
Because the estate isn't only Microsoft.

15
00:00:46.707 --> 00:00:50.649
Where an agency's HR platform runs
on Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

16
00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:55.020
a maintenance window on that platform
reaches the people who depend on it,

17
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.671
whether or not anyone
read the announcement.

18
00:00:57.671 --> 00:00:59.320
So the goal is concrete.

19
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.774
By the end of this chapter you can
read every announcement for a tenancy,

20
00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:07.096
the latest in each chain,
with its affected resources,

21
00:01:07.096 --> 00:01:10.720
into records that carry Oracle's
own action-required date.

22
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.846
Then the HR platform's
maintenance goes to the

23
00:01:13.846 --> 00:01:17.160
HR system's owners before
it lands, not after.

24
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.160
Next, which realm.

25
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.760
Oracle Cloud is divided into realms.

26
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.610
The commercial realm, O C
1, is what the lab uses.

27
00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:34.264
Oracle's US Government Cloud page lists
the Announcements service in realm O C 2,

28
00:01:34.264 --> 00:01:37.348
at two government regions,
Langley and Luke.

29
00:01:37.348 --> 00:01:42.181
For realm O C 3, Oracle's page wasn't
readable when the chapter was written,

30
00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:47.260
so its endpoint is not yet specified,
and the kit refuses it with that reason.

31
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.347
Why does it matter?

32
00:01:48.347 --> 00:01:52.298
A government tenancy read in the
commercial realm returns nothing.

33
00:01:52.298 --> 00:01:55.104
So the settings name the realm and region,

34
00:01:55.104 --> 00:01:58.260
and a region outside the realm is refused.

35
00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.760
Here's Oracle's starting point.

36
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.046
Oracle's CLI reference documents
the announcements list command,

37
00:02:08.046 --> 00:02:11.630
with a required compartment,
an option to read all pages,

38
00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.760
and an option to show only the
latest announcement in each chain.

39
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.946
Oracle's Announcement model gives
the fields: the type, two generic

40
00:02:19.946 --> 00:02:24.360
time fields each with its own type,
the lifecycle state and the chain ID.

41
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.988
A separate get command returns
one announcement's details,

42
00:02:27.988 --> 00:02:30.135
including its affected resources.

43
00:02:30.135 --> 00:02:34.240
Oracle's policy reference makes
announcements a resource type:

44
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.260
inspect lists summaries,
read adds the details.

45
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.152
And Oracle's common policy
is a single statement

46
00:02:40.152 --> 00:02:43.610
that lets a group read
announcements in the tenancy.

47
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.947
What does Oracle leave to you?

48
00:02:48.947 --> 00:02:53.198
Updates to one announcement form
a chain, so a rescheduled window

49
00:02:53.198 --> 00:02:57.685
arrives as a new announcement; read
every one and you keep the old date.

50
00:02:57.685 --> 00:03:00.747
Several announcement types
mean the same kind of news.

51
00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:03.457
The window and the deadline sit in generic

52
00:03:03.457 --> 00:03:06.398
time fields whose meaning
is in another field.

53
00:03:06.398 --> 00:03:09.734
The affected resources come
only from the details call.

54
00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:13.047
Oracle's reference doesn't
show the CLI's key casing.

55
00:03:13.047 --> 00:03:14.560
And the realms need a guard.

56
00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.585
Each gap is pinned by a test.

57
00:03:16.585 --> 00:03:20.300
The verdict: use Oracle's
commands and policy as published,

58
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.210
and build the chains, the time
fields and the realm guard.

59
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:28.898
Now the code.

60
00:03:28.898 --> 00:03:33.463
The collector makes the list call, then
a get for each announcement, because only

61
00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:38.098
the details call names the affected
resources and carries the full description.

62
00:03:38.098 --> 00:03:43.000
The normalizer keeps the latest announcement
in each chain, by its update time,

63
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.111
and gives the record the chain's ID,

64
00:03:45.111 --> 00:03:49.485
so a rescheduled window updates one
notice instead of adding a second.

65
00:03:49.485 --> 00:03:52.038
The summary is the
description from the details,

66
00:03:52.038 --> 00:03:54.400
an inactive announcement
closes the notice,

67
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.830
and the source link is the announcement
on the realm's own Announcements host.

68
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.618
Here's the mapping.

69
00:04:03.618 --> 00:04:08.179
A type that mentions maintenance is
maintenance; one that mentions change,

70
00:04:08.179 --> 00:04:12.594
or the action-required type, is a
change; a production event notification

71
00:04:12.594 --> 00:04:17.230
is an incident; and action-recommended
or information types are advisories.

72
00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:22.696
The time fields are read by their types: a
start or new start time opens the window,

73
00:04:22.696 --> 00:04:27.830
an end time closes it, and an action-required-by
time is Oracle's own deadline.

74
00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:33.930
Notice what's different from Azure and
Microsoft 365: Oracle flags action itself.

75
00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:38.252
So every action-required OCI
notice says the provider decided,

76
00:04:38.252 --> 00:04:40.940
and the kit needs no rule of its own.

77
00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:49.534
The tests cover the collector and the live
pieces: seven tests for the OCI block

78
00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:54.303
of the collectors, plus the live tests
for the list command and the realm guard.

79
00:04:54.303 --> 00:04:55.864
The lab runs nine of them.

80
00:04:55.864 --> 00:04:58.340
But be clear about what
this code isn't yet.

81
00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:02.161
The live reader was tested
against stand-in CLI output,

82
00:05:02.161 --> 00:05:06.915
not a live tenancy, so review it and
run Lab 2 before it reads a real one.

83
00:05:06.915 --> 00:05:09.728
And two things are
still not yet specified:

84
00:05:09.728 --> 00:05:13.987
which key casing the CLI actually
prints, so the kit accepts both,

85
00:05:13.987 --> 00:05:18.680
and the Announcements endpoint in
realm O C 3, so the kit refuses it.

86
00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:23.480
Who's involved?

87
00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.592
Developers maintain the
collector and the realm guard.

88
00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.381
The OCI platform lead owns this gate.

89
00:05:29.381 --> 00:05:34.380
The owners of systems on OCI, an HR platform
among them where the agency has one,

90
00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.818
receive the maintenance
that names their resources.

91
00:05:37.818 --> 00:05:41.593
And the security officer keeps
the policy and the run folder.

92
00:05:41.593 --> 00:05:46.530
What the reader may hold is one policy
statement: read announcements in the tenancy.

93
00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:51.055
Inspect is enough to list, but read
is what the details call needs.

94
00:05:51.055 --> 00:05:55.228
Grant only inspect and you get
summaries without affected resources,

95
00:05:55.228 --> 00:05:58.180
so nothing routes as
naming your resources.

96
00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:06.042
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder.

97
00:06:06.042 --> 00:06:10.357
The lab settings name a fictional
tenancy in the commercial realm,

98
00:06:10.357 --> 00:06:12.867
in the Ashburn region; nothing signs in.

99
00:06:12.867 --> 00:06:18.056
Step 1 lists what OCI needs: inspect
to list, read for the details.

100
00:06:18.056 --> 00:06:22.182
Step 2 shows the realms the kit
knows: the commercial domain,

101
00:06:22.182 --> 00:06:27.405
the government domain with its two regions,
and O C 3 refused with its reason.

102
00:06:27.405 --> 00:06:33.260
That refusal is deliberate: an endpoint
nobody could confirm is never guessed.

103
00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.253
Step 3 collects OCI from the
day-one fixtures: four notices,

104
00:06:41.253 --> 00:06:45.585
three open, one needing action,
all valid against the schema.

105
00:06:45.585 --> 00:06:47.585
Step 4 reads the records.

106
00:06:47.585 --> 00:06:51.635
Look at the resources column: that's why
the kit makes a get per announcement.

107
00:06:51.635 --> 00:06:56.735
The maintenance names a database system,
with its window starting November 22.

108
00:06:56.735 --> 00:07:01.963
The change names a load balancer, and
it's action required by December 15,

109
00:07:01.963 --> 00:07:04.710
by Oracle's own type, not by any kit rule.

110
00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:08.375
The advisory is open, and the
incident is already closed,

111
00:07:08.375 --> 00:07:10.685
because Oracle marked it inactive.

112
00:07:10.685 --> 00:07:13.365
Because both name the agency's resources,

113
00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:17.510
Chapter 6 can route each one to
the owners of those systems.

114
00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:25.198
Step 5 collects the second week,
and this is the step to watch.

115
00:07:25.198 --> 00:07:27.534
Oracle rescheduled the maintenance.

116
00:07:27.534 --> 00:07:31.885
In Oracle's model, that arrives as a
new announcement in the same chain.

117
00:07:31.885 --> 00:07:36.225
The kit keeps the chain's latest,
so the database maintenance now

118
00:07:36.225 --> 00:07:40.647
starts November 29 instead of
November 22, with its new window end.

119
00:07:40.647 --> 00:07:43.291
And it's still one
notice, with the same ID,

120
00:07:43.291 --> 00:07:47.935
so Chapter 6 updates the existing
task instead of opening a second one.

121
00:07:47.935 --> 00:07:50.172
Read every announcement on its own,

122
00:07:50.172 --> 00:07:53.690
and the old window would
linger next to the new one.

123
00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.002
Step 6 builds the live list
command without running it.

124
00:08:01.002 --> 00:08:05.419
These are the exact arguments a live
run would pass to Oracle's CLI:

125
00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:10.162
the tenancy as the compartment, all
pages, only the latest in each chain,

126
00:08:10.162 --> 00:08:13.352
the region from the
settings, and JSON output.

127
00:08:13.352 --> 00:08:17.753
Compare them with Oracle's command
reference: they're Oracle's own options.

128
00:08:17.753 --> 00:08:22.278
Step 7 runs this chapter's
tests: nine pass, none fail.

129
00:08:22.278 --> 00:08:25.899
The raw folder holds the list
and each announcement's details,

130
00:08:25.899 --> 00:08:28.314
which is what the security officer keeps.

131
00:08:28.314 --> 00:08:30.880
Delete the output folder when you're done.

132
00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:38.455
Lab 2 uses an OCI test
tenancy, never production.

133
00:08:38.455 --> 00:08:43.574
As an IAM administrator, add Oracle's one
policy statement for the reader's group,

134
00:08:43.574 --> 00:08:46.598
from a file so the shell
doesn't touch its quotes,

135
00:08:46.598 --> 00:08:49.080
and keep the policy's ID for the back-out.

136
00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.781
Configure the CLI as the reader
with Oracle's setup command,

137
00:08:52.781 --> 00:08:54.442
which creates the key pair.

138
00:08:54.442 --> 00:08:58.531
Then cross-check: Oracle's own list
command counts the announcements,

139
00:08:58.531 --> 00:09:00.755
and the kit's collection should agree.

140
00:09:00.755 --> 00:09:05.742
Record the key casing the CLI printed,
because Oracle's reference doesn't show it.

141
00:09:05.742 --> 00:09:11.290
Finally, delete the policy and the run,
which names the tenancy's resources.

142
00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.840
Each output is evidence for something
specific, against NIST's control catalog.

143
00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.103
A policy that lets the reader's
group read announcements and

144
00:09:23.103 --> 00:09:26.627
nothing else supports monitoring
an external service provider.

145
00:09:26.627 --> 00:09:30.546
A run folder showing every chain's
latest announcement was read,

146
00:09:30.546 --> 00:09:35.427
in the right realm, supports that and
receiving security alerts and advisories.

147
00:09:35.427 --> 00:09:40.320
Maintenance that names the agency's
resources supports contingency planning,

148
00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.878
because Chapter 6 asks the
system owner to review the plan.

149
00:09:43.878 --> 00:09:48.439
And a rescheduled window that stays one
notice supports continuous monitoring.

150
00:09:48.439 --> 00:09:52.365
The indicators are FedRAMP's;
the mapping is the program's.

151
00:09:52.365 --> 00:09:54.890
The assessor decides the controls.

152
00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.040
What breaks in production?

153
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:04.805
Grant inspect instead of read and you
get summaries without affected resources,

154
00:10:04.805 --> 00:10:07.365
so nothing routes as
naming your resources.

155
00:10:07.365 --> 00:10:10.252
Read every announcement
in a chain on its own

156
00:10:10.252 --> 00:10:13.140
and the old window lingers
next to the new one.

157
00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:17.340
Read a government tenancy in the
commercial realm and nothing comes back.

158
00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:21.850
Let only the help desk see the HR
platform's maintenance and its owners learn

159
00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:26.002
of it after it lands, so the workbook's
design guidance routes it to them.

160
00:10:26.002 --> 00:10:30.621
And treat the email as the record and it
can be spoofed and isn't in the evidence;

161
00:10:30.621 --> 00:10:33.640
the task links the
announcement the kit read.

162
00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:38.740
Here's what done looks like.

163
00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:42.740
The policy grants read announcements to
the reader's group, and nothing more.

164
00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:46.079
The settings name the
realm and the home region,

165
00:10:46.079 --> 00:10:48.477
and a government tenancy uses O C 2.

166
00:10:48.477 --> 00:10:52.339
Oracle's list and the kit's
collection agree in the test tenancy.

167
00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:55.552
Maintenance notices name
the affected resources.

168
00:10:55.552 --> 00:10:57.764
The key casing you saw is recorded.

169
00:10:57.764 --> 00:10:59.881
And the owners of systems on OCI,

170
00:10:59.881 --> 00:11:04.440
an HR platform among them where the
agency has one, are in the routing.

171
00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.528
Next, Chapter 4 fills the
same record from AWS Health.

172
00:11:08.528 --> 00:11:09.690
Thanks for watching.
